WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en
Style:
::cue(c.cyan) { color: cyan; }
::cue(c.yellow) { color: yellow; }
##

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.580 line:91%
In England, if you want to sell
alcohol to the public,

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.020 line:85%
you need a licence.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.680 line:91%
You can't just set up
a bar or a pub anywhere.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:11.080 line:91%
And the law about that is set down
in the Licensing Act 2003.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:14.800 position:56% line:91%
There are a lot of details in there,
including some exceptions.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.440 position:61% line:91%
For example, section 173 makes it clear
that as long as you're "on a journey",

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.840 position:64% line:91%
you do not need a licence to sell alcohol
aboard a train, a plane...

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.320 line:85%
..or a hovercraft.

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:27.711 line:85%
<c.cyan>Anybody can go fast,</c>

00:00:27.711 --> 00:00:30.714 position:59% line:91%
<c.cyan>but when you come to the first corner,
that's when you're gonna get ejected.</c>

00:00:30.714 --> 00:00:33.650 position:59% line:91%
<c.cyan>It's like drifting cars.
It's all about steering, a hovercraft.</c>

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:35.853 line:91%
<c.cyan>Not about going fast,
it's learning how to steer them.</c>

00:00:35.853 --> 00:00:39.957 position:77% line:91%
<c.yellow>The thing is with hovercraft:
if you're looking at it, you're likely to hit it!</c>

00:00:39.957 --> 00:00:43.794 line:91%
<c.yellow>In a hovercraft, to fly them,
you do lean with them,​</c>

00:00:43.794 --> 00:00:46.096 line:91%
<c.yellow>which makes the craft pitch
and makes it turn.​​</c>

00:00:46.096 --> 00:00:49.299 position:66% line:91%
<c.yellow>So, if you're serving out the back of one,
and you lean to give it to someone,</c>

00:00:49.299 --> 00:00:53.203 line:91%
<c.yellow>might cause some interesting...
control of the hovercraft,</c>

00:00:53.203 --> 00:00:55.172 line:85%
<c.yellow>but it's all part of the fun(!)</c>

00:00:55.172 --> 00:00:57.041 line:91%
<c.cyan>Yeah, you'll be fine
in the back of there.​</c>

00:00:57.041 --> 00:00:59.676 position:58% line:91%
<c.cyan>We'll just keep it straight as we can
and serve your customers.</c>

00:00:59.676 --> 00:01:02.513 position:59% line:91%
And you might think: "Tom, you're just
doing this for show, aren't you?

00:01:02.513 --> 00:01:05.582 align:start position:0% line:91%
"You're not actually selling alcohol
to people?" Wrong!

00:01:05.582 --> 00:01:08.986 position:58% line:91%
This is a real beer pump.
There is real beer being fed into it.

00:01:08.986 --> 00:01:12.389 align:start size:88% position:13% line:91%
And this is a working
contactless card reader.

00:01:12.389 --> 00:01:16.493 align:start position:0% line:91%
It is connected to a company bank account.
It is absolutely taking payments.

00:01:17.227 --> 00:01:19.329 line:85%
Right. Let's do this.

00:01:45.656 --> 00:01:48.192 line:91%
<i>I did try to do my piece to camera
on the hovercraft.​​</i>

00:01:48.192 --> 00:01:49.226 line:85%
<i>You couldn't hear a thing.</i>

00:01:49.226 --> 00:01:51.695 line:91%
<i>So I'm gonna explain in voiceover
how this all worked,​​</i>

00:01:51.695 --> 00:01:53.597 line:91%
<i>in between the bits
where I have to shout at people.</i>

00:01:57.267 --> 00:02:00.270 position:58% line:91%
<i>Full disclosure: there are only
trusted friends buying alcohol today,</i>

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:03.607 position:77% line:91%
<i>both for safety, and because while
the selling alcohol part does appear to be legal,</i>

00:02:03.607 --> 00:02:06.043 position:56% line:91%
<i>I'm pretty sure there are
several other Trading Standards laws</i>

00:02:06.043 --> 00:02:07.478 line:85%
<i>that I'm only vaguely following.</i>

00:02:07.478 --> 00:02:08.979 line:85%
<i>Don't take this as legal advice.</i>

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:11.281 position:56% line:91%
<i>But yes, the contactless card reader
really did work.​ ​</i>

00:02:11.281 --> 00:02:14.051 position:72% line:91%
<i>And yes, that is a terrible half pint of beer.
There was a sparkler along the tap,</i>

00:02:14.051 --> 00:02:16.487 position:56% line:91%
<i>and the whole thing had been jostled
and vibrated by the hovercraft.</i>

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.678 line:91%
<i>Yeah, that should've been
the first clue that I misheard him.</i>

00:02:41.678 --> 00:02:45.649 position:67% line:91%
<i>£1.50 was not the price of a gin and tonic.
It was the price of a half pint.</i>

00:02:45.649 --> 00:02:48.585 line:91%
<i>Another terrible half pint
that's mostly foam.​ ​</i>

00:02:48.585 --> 00:02:51.989 position:59% line:91%
<i>So as Marc the pilot tried desperately
to counterbalance me...</i>

00:03:01.431 --> 00:03:05.469 position:70% line:91%
<i>I genuinely don't know if he was expecting me
to take that glass back somehow, but...</i>

00:03:05.936 --> 00:03:09.173 position:69% line:91%
<i>Yeah, we picked that up later.
Most of the foam from it got pulled onto me.</i>

00:03:09.173 --> 00:03:11.909 line:91%
<i>Oh, and watch the plastic glass
between my knees there.</i>

00:03:11.909 --> 00:03:13.911 line:91%
<i>Yeah, we picked that up
later too.​</i>

00:03:13.911 --> 00:03:16.079 position:58% line:91%
<i>Note that I'm using a thimble measure
for the gin there.​​</i>

00:03:16.079 --> 00:03:19.049 position:64% line:91%
<i>Free pour of spirits for drinks like that
isn't legal in the UK.​</i>

00:03:19.049 --> 00:03:22.152 position:69% line:91%
<i>You're fine if it's a complicated cocktail,
but for something simple like gin and tonic,</i>

00:03:22.152 --> 00:03:24.154 position:56% line:85%
<i>legally, you have to measure it out.</i>

00:03:24.154 --> 00:03:26.657 position:61% line:91%
<i>And remember, we've got to keep moving,
at least slightly.​​</i>

00:03:26.657 --> 00:03:29.726 position:59% line:91%
<i>The exemption only works
if the hovercraft is "on a journey"...</i>

00:03:29.726 --> 00:03:32.663 position:59% line:91%
<i>even if that journey is back and forth
across a hovercraft racetrack.</i>

00:03:39.336 --> 00:03:41.038 line:91%
<i>I did not bring
a change of clothes.​​</i>

00:03:41.038 --> 00:03:43.207 line:91%
<i>I drove home smelling faintly
of gin and tonic.​​</i>

00:03:53.917 --> 00:03:57.654 line:91%
<i>Yeah, the hover martini was just
gin and vodka on ice.​</i>

00:03:57.654 --> 00:03:59.590 line:91%
<i>And some grass that got kicked up
by the hovercraft's fans.</i>

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:01.358 line:85%
<i>I am not a good bartender.</i>

00:04:01.358 --> 00:04:05.495 position:72% line:91%
<i>And I am amazed by how good Marc the pilot
was at flying the hovercraft in a strong wind.</i>

00:04:05.495 --> 00:04:08.065 line:91%
<i>He's a hovercraft racer, along with
Rob who helped set all this up.</i>

00:04:08.065 --> 00:04:11.168 position:59% line:91%
<i>And this is a track that is all curves
and no straightaways.​</i>

00:04:12.636 --> 00:04:15.105 line:91%
<i>There... yeah, there was
a lot of grass in it.​</i>

00:04:15.105 --> 00:04:17.808 line:91%
<i>So, the big question:
Why does this law exist?</i>

00:04:17.808 --> 00:04:20.477 position:61% line:85%
<i>Why is there this ridiculous exemption?</i>

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:29.653 line:91%
<i>Alcohol licences are granted by
local authorities in England,</i>

00:04:29.653 --> 00:04:31.888 position:56% line:91%
<i>and trains and planes travel through
the whole country.​​</i>

00:04:31.888 --> 00:04:34.391 line:91%
<i>It's meant to cover
selling alcohol to passengers.</i>

00:04:34.391 --> 00:04:36.893 position:70% line:91%
<i>And while these days, there's only one
hovercraft service, and it takes ten minutes,</i>

00:04:36.893 --> 00:04:39.930 line:91%
<i>we used to have massive hovercraft
that'd cross the Channel to France.</i>

00:04:44.735 --> 00:04:47.337 line:91%
<i>The law hasn't been updated,
because it doesn't need to be.</i>

00:04:47.337 --> 00:04:48.205 line:85%
<i>After all...</i>

00:04:49.439 --> 00:04:52.876 position:64% line:91%
<i>Who's gonna be ridiculous enough
to sell alcohol from a moving hovercraft?</i>

00:05:11.161 --> 00:05:14.164 align:start size:88% position:13% line:8%
